---
// Ported from a standalone React/Tailwind prototype (back-of-the-envelope.jsx).
// This project doesn't use React or Tailwind, so the port is a rewrite onto
// plain Astro markup + vanilla JS + this site's own CSS tokens, following the
// same "retheme the chrome, keep the math untouched" approach used for the
// SLAM and Hybrid A* Parking sims. Traffic and Diagram are the two tabs built
// so far. The source calculator has 13 sections total (Storage, Bandwidth,
// Cache, Compute, Database, Queue, Latency, Availability, Cost, Toolbox,
// Cheat sheet, Summary); more get added a couple at a time as their own tabs.
// The Diagram tab is a small original addition, not from the source file: a
// vanilla-JS canvas for sketching an architecture (drag boxes, connect them,
// label, pan/zoom), autosaved to localStorage with a JSON export. No preset
// scenarios anywhere in this tool, by request.
---

<div class="boe-root">
  <div class="boe-head">
    <div>
      <p class="boe-blurb" id="boeBlurb">Capacity math for system design. Change one assumption and watch it move through the numbers below.</p>
    </div>
    <button type="button" id="boeReset" class="boe-reset">Reset</button>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tabs">
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn active" data-tab="traffic">Traffic</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="storage">Storage</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="cache">Cache</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="bandwidth">Bandwidth</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="compute">Compute</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="database">Database</button>
    <button type="button" class="boe-tabbtn" data-tab="diagram">Diagram</button>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="traffic">
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Inputs</h3>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Daily active users<span class="boe-unit">DAU</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="dau" value="10000000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="dauPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">People who hit the system in a day.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Requests per user per day<span class="boe-unit">req</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="rpu" value="20">
            <span class="boe-hint">Count API calls, not screens.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Peak-to-average factor</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="peakVal">3</span>×</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="1" max="20" step="0.5" id="peak" value="3">
            <span class="boe-hint">Traffic is never flat. 2–4× for global apps, 10×+ for sales and live events.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Reads per write<span class="boe-unit">: 1</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="rw" value="100">
            <span class="boe-hint">100 means 100 reads for every write.</span>
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>Everything downstream hangs off peak RPS, so get it first. The only constant you need is <strong>86,400 seconds in a day</strong>, round it to 100,000 and your numbers land within 15%, which is well inside the error bars of every other guess you are making.</p>
          <p>Size for <strong>peak</strong>, not average. A system sized for the mean is down every evening.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Derived load</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Peak requests per second</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-peakRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak = DAU × req/user ÷ 86,400 × peak factor</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Average RPS</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-rps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">DAU × req/user ÷ 86,400</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Requests per day</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-reqDay">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">DAU × req/user</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Requests per month</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-reqMonth">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">× 30</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Read / write split</h3>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Peak read RPS</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-peakReadRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula" id="out-rShare">—</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Peak write RPS</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-peakWriteRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula" id="out-wShare">—</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Writes per day</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-writesDay">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-sub">Feeds the storage tab</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Reads per day</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-readsDay">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-scale">
          Above ~100k RPS you are past what a single region and a single primary database will do comfortably. Expect sharding, regional partitioning and an edge tier in your design.
        </div>
        <div class="boe-flag boe-flag-ok" id="flag-readheavy">
          Read-heavy. This is a caching and read-replica problem. Writes are almost a rounding error, so spend your design budget on the read path.
        </div>
        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-writeheavy">
          Write-heavy. Caching will not save you. Look at LSM-tree stores, batching, and a log in front of the database.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="storage" hidden>
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Inputs</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="stLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use writes/day from Traffic</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="stLinkedNote">Currently <span id="stLinkedValue">—</span> writes per day, linked from Traffic.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="stWritesField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Writes per day<span class="boe-unit">obj</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="stWrites" value="2000000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="stWritesPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Bytes per object<span class="boe-unit">B</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="wbytes" value="1024">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="wbytesPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">Payload plus row overhead. A tweet is around 300 B; a photo is around 2 MB.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Compression ratio<span class="boe-unit">: 1</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="comp" value="2">
            <span class="boe-hint">Text compresses 2–5×, JSON 4–10×, already-compressed media around 1×.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Index &amp; metadata overhead</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="idxPctVal">25</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="200" step="5" id="idxPct" value="25">
            <span class="boe-hint">Secondary indexes often cost more than the rows themselves.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Replication factor<span class="boe-unit">×</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="repl" value="3">
            <span class="boe-hint">3 is the default for durability across availability zones.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Retention<span class="boe-unit">years</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="years" value="5">
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Yearly growth</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="growthVal">30</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="200" step="5" id="growth" value="30">
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>Storage is a <strong>flow</strong> problem, not a stock problem: bytes per day, times days. The two multipliers people forget sit on either side of that: compression divides the size, while indexes and replication multiply it back up.</p>
          <p>With growth <em>g</em>, cumulative years form a geometric series, not a straight multiply: <code>Y1 × ((1+g)^N − 1) / g</code>. At 30% growth, five years comes out to 9.0 first-years, not 5.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Provisioned storage</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Total after <span id="stYearsLabel">5</span> years</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-physical">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">logical × replication</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Logical (one copy)</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-logical">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">Y1 × ((1+g)^N − 1) / g</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>First-year footprint</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-y1">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">daily × 365</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Per day (raw)</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-rawDay">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">writes/day × bytes</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Per day (stored, all copies)</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-physDay">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">raw ÷ compression × (1+index) × replicas</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Per month (stored)</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-physMonth">—</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Objects stored</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-objects">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-sub">Row and key count matters as much as bytes for index design</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-petabyte">
          Petabyte scale. Object storage with a metadata index beats a general-purpose database here, and cold tiering will dominate your cost conversation.
        </div>
        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-billionrows">
          Over 100 billion rows. No single index fits in memory, so plan for partitioned indexes or key-value access patterns only.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="cache" hidden>
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Inputs</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="cacheLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use peak read RPS from Traffic</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="cacheLinkedNote">Currently <span id="cacheLinkedValue">—</span> peak read RPS, linked from Traffic.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="cacheReadsField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Peak read requests/sec<span class="boe-unit">RPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cacheReads" value="5000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="cacheReadsPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Cache hit ratio</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="hitRatioVal">80</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1" id="hitRatio" value="80">
            <span class="boe-hint">Share of reads the cache answers without touching the database.</span>
          </label>

          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="cacheDataLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use total data size from Storage</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="cacheDataLinkedNote">Currently <span id="cacheDataLinkedValue">—</span> of data, linked from Storage.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="cacheDataField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Total data size<span class="boe-unit">GB</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cacheDataManual" value="500">
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Working set, % of total data</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="workingPctVal">20</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1" id="workingPct" value="20">
            <span class="boe-hint">Real access patterns are skewed: a small hot slice serves most reads. That slice, not your whole dataset, is what has to fit in memory.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Average cached item size<span class="boe-unit">B</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cacheItemBytes" value="2048">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="cacheItemBytesPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">Usually bigger than a raw row: a cache entry is often a rendered or joined response.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Memory overhead<span class="boe-unit">: 1</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="cacheOverhead" value="1.3">
            <span class="boe-hint">Key overhead, hash table structure, fragmentation. Redis alone runs 1.2–1.5×.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Replicas per shard<span class="boe-unit">×</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="cacheRepl" value="2">
            <span class="boe-hint">At least one replica for failover; more if reads need to scale past a single node.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Usable memory per node<span class="boe-unit">GB</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cacheNodeGB" value="24">
            <span class="boe-hint">Leave headroom below the instance's advertised RAM. A 32 GB box is not 32 GB of usable cache.</span>
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>A cache only ever helps the <strong>read</strong> path, and only for data that gets read again before it changes. It doesn't reduce how much data exists, it reduces how often you have to go get it, which is why the two numbers that matter are the hit ratio (how much traffic it absorbs) and the working set (how much of the data has to stay hot to earn that hit ratio).</p>
          <p>Real traffic is skewed, not uniform: a small slice of keys usually accounts for most reads. That's what makes caching cheap, you're not memory-provisioning for the whole dataset, just its hot tail. Get the working-set estimate wrong, low, and you'll see a hit ratio that never reaches what you sized for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Cache traffic</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Reads served by cache</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheHitRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak read RPS × hit ratio</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Reads that still reach the database</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheMissRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak read RPS × (1 − hit ratio)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Backend load</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheReduction">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Cache sizing</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Cache nodes needed</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheNodes">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">memory needed ÷ usable memory per node</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Memory needed, with replicas</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheMemTotal">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">raw × replicas</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Memory needed, raw</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheMemRaw">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">working set × overhead</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Working set</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-workingSet">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">total data × working-set %</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Cached items (keys)</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-cacheItems">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-sub">Roughly how many keys you're managing, not just bytes</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-cache-lowhit">
          Under 50% hit ratio, the cache is barely earning its keep. Check the eviction policy and TTLs, or whether this access pattern is cacheable at all, before adding nodes to fix what might be a modeling problem.
        </div>
        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-cache-fleet">
          That's a large fleet for one logical cache. Look at a managed cluster mode that shards across nodes, and double check whether the whole working set really needs to be hot at once.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="bandwidth" hidden>
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Inputs</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="bwLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use peak RPS from Traffic</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="bwLinkedNote">Currently <span id="bwLinkedValue">—</span> peak reads/sec and <span id="bwLinkedWriteValue">—</span> peak writes/sec, linked from Traffic.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="bwReadsField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Peak read requests/sec<span class="boe-unit">RPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="bwReads" value="6000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="bwReadsPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field" id="bwWritesField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Peak write requests/sec<span class="boe-unit">RPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="bwWrites" value="60">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="bwWritesPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Average response size<span class="boe-unit">B</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="respBytes" value="5120">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="respBytesPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">What actually goes out the wire: JSON payload, headers, TLS overhead. Usually bigger than the raw object.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Average request size<span class="boe-unit">B</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="reqBytes" value="1024">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="reqBytesPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">Upload or write payload plus headers.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">CDN / edge offload</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="cdnPctVal">60</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1" id="cdnPct" value="60">
            <span class="boe-hint">Static assets and edge-cacheable responses that never reach your origin at all. This is a different lever from the Cache tab's hit ratio: caching saves your database, a CDN saves your network. 0% if you have no CDN.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Protocol overhead<span class="boe-unit">: 1</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="bwOverhead" value="1.15">
            <span class="boe-hint">TCP/TLS handshakes, retries, and headers on top of the payload. 10–20% is typical.</span>
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>Bandwidth has the same peak-vs-average split as everything else in this tool, but it shows up twice: the <strong>peak</strong> figure is what you provision, the link speed and NIC capacity that has to survive your busiest second. The <strong>monthly transfer</strong> figure is what you get billed for, built from average traffic over the whole month, not the peak.</p>
          <p>CDN offload and cache hit ratio look similar but solve different problems: caching keeps repeat reads off your database while still serving them from your own servers, so they still count as egress here. A CDN in front of that serves some of those reads from the edge instead, so they never touch your origin's network at all, that's the traffic this tab actually removes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Peak bandwidth</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Total, at peak</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwTotal">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">egress + ingress</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Egress, reads out</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwEgress">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">origin read RPS × response size × overhead</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Ingress, writes in</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwIngress">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak write RPS × request size × overhead</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Origin read RPS after CDN</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwOriginRps">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak read RPS × (1 − CDN offload)</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Monthly transfer</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Total per month</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwTotalMonth">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">average volume, not peak</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Egress per month</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwEgressMonth">—</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Ingress per month</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-bwIngressMonth">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-bandwidth-heavy">
          Multiple Gbps at peak is past what a single instance's NIC handles comfortably. Expect a load-balancer tier fanning out across several boxes, not one box with a bigger pipe.
        </div>
        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-bandwidth-cost">
          At this monthly volume, egress pricing is likely a bigger line item than compute. A CDN, or a cloud provider with cheap/free egress, pays for itself here.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="compute" hidden>
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Inputs</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="cpLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use peak RPS from Traffic</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="cpLinkedNote">Currently <span id="cpLinkedValue">—</span> peak RPS, linked from Traffic.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="cpReqField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Peak requests/sec<span class="boe-unit">RPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cpReq" value="7000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="cpReqPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">CPU time per request<span class="boe-unit">ms</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="cpuMs" value="20">
            <span class="boe-hint">Actual processor work, excluding time spent waiting on I/O.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">End-to-end service time<span class="boe-unit">ms</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="svcMs" value="80">
            <span class="boe-hint">Wall clock inside your service, including downstream waits. Drives concurrency.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Cores per server<span class="boe-unit">vCPU</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="any" id="cores" value="8">
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Target CPU utilisation</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="utilVal">60</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="10" max="95" step="5" id="util" value="60">
            <span class="boe-hint">Above ~70% queueing delay starts to dominate tail latency.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Availability zones<span class="boe-unit">AZ</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="1" step="1" id="az" value="3">
            <span class="boe-hint">Provision so the loss of one zone still carries peak.</span>
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>Two different laws, two different answers, and mixing them up is the classic mistake. <strong>Throughput</strong> is bounded by CPU work: a core does 1000 ÷ cpu-ms requests per second. <strong>Concurrency</strong> is Little's Law, <code>L = λ × W</code>, a request waiting 200ms on a database still occupies a thread even though it burns no CPU. That's why an I/O-heavy service needs many more threads than cores.</p>
          <p>Utilisation is where queueing theory bites. For an M/M/1 queue, latency scales as <code>1 / (1 − ρ)</code>. At 50% load you wait 2× service time; at 90% you wait 10×; at 99% you wait 100×. Nothing changed about the server, only how full it is.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Fleet size</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Servers to provision</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-serversAz">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">minimum × AZ ÷ (AZ − 1), for zone loss</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Servers to serve peak</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-servers">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak RPS ÷ (cores × 1000/cpu-ms × utilisation)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Throughput per server</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-perServer">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula" id="out-perServerFormula">—</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Fleet headroom at peak</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-headroom">—<span class="boe-out-unit">RPS</span></span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Concurrency (Little's Law)</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>In-flight requests at peak</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-concurrency">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">L = peak RPS × service time</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Threads or connections per server</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-threadsPerServer">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-sub">Set your pool at least this high, or you queue before you even start work</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Latency multiplier at target load</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-qMult">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">1 / (1 − ρ)</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Utilisation vs latency</h3>
          <div class="boe-table-wrap">
            <table class="boe-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Utilisation</th><th>Latency ×</th><th>Servers needed</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody id="utilRefBody"></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-compute-util">
          Running above 80% leaves nothing for a deploy, a GC pause or a retry storm. The servers you save show up as p99 latency.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="database" hidden>
    <div class="boe-grid">
      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Dataset</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="dbRecordsLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use record count from Storage</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="dbRecordsLinkedNote">Currently <span id="dbRecordsLinkedValue">—</span> objects stored, linked from Storage.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="dbRecordsField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Total records<span class="boe-unit">rows</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="dbRecords" value="20000000000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="dbRecordsPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Bytes per record<span class="boe-unit">B</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="recBytes" value="512">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="recBytesPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label-row">
              <span class="boe-label-text">Index overhead</span>
              <span class="boe-slide-val"><span id="dbIdxVal">30</span>%</span>
            </span>
            <input class="boe-range" type="range" min="0" max="200" step="5" id="dbIdx" value="30">
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Shard limits</h3>
          <label class="boe-toggle">
            <input type="checkbox" id="dbQpsLink" checked>
            <span class="boe-toggle-track"><span class="boe-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
            <span class="boe-toggle-label">Use peak RPS from Traffic</span>
          </label>
          <div class="boe-linked-note" id="dbQpsLinkedNote">Currently <span id="dbQpsLinkedValue">—</span> peak RPS, linked from Traffic.</div>
          <label class="boe-field" id="dbQpsField" hidden>
            <span class="boe-label">Peak queries/sec<span class="boe-unit">QPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="dbQps" value="7000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="dbQpsPreview"></span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Storage per shard<span class="boe-unit">GB</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="shardCapGB" value="2000">
            <span class="boe-hint">Keep it small enough to rebuild, back up and rebalance in reasonable time. 1–2 TB is a common ceiling.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">QPS per shard<span class="boe-unit">QPS</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="shardQps" value="20000">
            <span class="boe-num-preview" id="shardQpsPreview"></span>
            <span class="boe-hint">Postgres primary ~10–20k simple queries/s; Cassandra node ~10k writes/s.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="boe-field">
            <span class="boe-label">Replicas per shard<span class="boe-unit">×</span></span>
            <input class="boe-inp" type="number" min="0" step="any" id="dbRepl" value="2">
          </label>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-learn">
          <div class="boe-learn-label">Why it works</div>
          <p>Shard count is the <strong>maximum</strong> of two independent constraints: how much data fits, and how much traffic a node takes. Sizing on storage alone is how you end up with fat, idle shards that still fall over.</p>
          <p>Averages lie about shards. A uniform split assumes uniform keys, and real keys are Zipfian, one celebrity, one popular product, one busy tenant. Estimate your hottest shard at several times the mean and check that number instead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Sharding</h3>
          <div class="boe-out boe-out-big">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Shards</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-shards">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">max(storage-bound, QPS-bound)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Storage-bound shards</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-shardsStorage">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">dataset ÷ per-shard capacity</div>
            <div class="boe-sub" id="out-shardsStorageSub"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>QPS-bound shards</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-shardsQps">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula">peak QPS ÷ per-shard QPS</div>
            <div class="boe-sub" id="out-shardsQpsSub"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Total nodes with replicas</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-dbNodes">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-formula" id="out-dbNodesFormula">—</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-panel">
          <h3 class="boe-panel-title">Per shard</h3>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Data per shard</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-perShardBytes">—</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Rows per shard</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-perShardRows">—</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>QPS per shard, uniform</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-perShardQps">—</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Hottest shard at 5× skew</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-hotShardQps">—</span></div>
            <div class="boe-sub" id="out-hotShardQpsSub"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="boe-out">
            <div class="boe-out-row"><span>Total dataset with indexes</span><span class="boe-out-val" id="out-dataset">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="boe-flag" id="flag-database-shards">
          Over a thousand shards means rebalancing, schema migrations and cross-shard queries are now their own engineering programme. Consider a bigger per-shard budget first.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="boe-tab" data-tab="diagram" hidden>
    <div class="dg-root">
      <div class="dg-toolbar">
        <div class="dg-chip" id="dgAddBox" draggable="true" title="Drag onto the canvas, or click to add">+ Box</div>
        <div class="dg-chip" id="dgAddContainer" draggable="true" title="Drag onto the canvas, or click to add">+ Container</div>
        <div class="dg-chip" id="dgAddText" draggable="true" title="Drag onto the canvas, or click to add">+ Text</div>
        <button type="button" class="dg-chip dg-chip-btn" id="dgAddComponent" title="Pick a labeled component">+ Component ▾</button>
        <div class="dg-tool-group">
          <button type="button" class="dg-btn" id="dgZoomOut" aria-label="Zoom out">−</button>
          <span class="dg-zoom-val" id="dgZoomVal">100%</span>
          <button type="button" class="dg-btn" id="dgZoomIn" aria-label="Zoom in">+</button>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgZoomReset" title="Reset view" aria-label="Reset view">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 10a6 6 0 1 1 1.8 4.3"/><path d="M4 14v-4h4"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgGroup" disabled title="Group" aria-label="Group">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="10" height="10" rx="1.2"/><rect x="3" y="3" width="10" height="10" rx="1.2" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgUngroup" disabled title="Ungroup" aria-label="Ungroup">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/><rect x="10" y="10" width="7" height="7" rx="1"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgToFront" disabled title="Bring to front" aria-label="Bring to front">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 11l5-5 5 5"/><path d="M5 16l5-5 5 5"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgToBack" disabled title="Send to back" aria-label="Send to back">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 4l5 5 5-5"/><path d="M5 9l5 5 5-5"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgDuplicate" disabled title="Duplicate" aria-label="Duplicate">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="5" width="10" height="10" rx="1.2"/><rect x="7" y="2" width="10" height="10" rx="1.2"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgUndo" disabled title="Undo" aria-label="Undo">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 5 3 8l3 3"/><path d="M3 8h8a5 5 0 0 1 0 10h-2"/></svg>
        </button>
        <span class="dg-toolbar-spacer"></span>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgFullscreen" title="Fullscreen" aria-label="Fullscreen">
          <svg class="dg-icon dg-icon-enter" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8V3h5"/><path d="M17 8V3h-5"/><path d="M3 12v5h5"/><path d="M17 12v5h-5"/></svg>
          <svg class="dg-icon dg-icon-exit" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" hidden><path d="M8 3v5H3"/><path d="M12 3v5h5"/><path d="M8 17v-5H3"/><path d="M12 17v-5h5"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgExport" title="Export JSON" aria-label="Export JSON">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 3v9"/><path d="M6 8l4 4 4-4"/><path d="M3 15v2h14v-2"/></svg>
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon" id="dgImport" title="Import JSON" aria-label="Import JSON">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 13V4"/><path d="M6 8l4-4 4 4"/><path d="M3 15v2h14v-2"/></svg>
        </button>
        <input type="file" id="dgImportInput" accept="application/json,.json" hidden>
        <button type="button" class="dg-btn dg-btn-icon dg-btn-danger" id="dgClear" title="Clear" aria-label="Clear">
          <svg class="dg-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 6h12"/><path d="M8 6V4h4v2"/><path d="M6 6l1 10a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h4a1 1 0 0 0 1-1l1-10"/><path d="M8.5 9v4"/><path d="M11.5 9v4"/></svg>
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="dg-canvas-wrap" id="dgCanvasWrap">
        <div class="dg-viewport" id="dgViewport">
          <svg class="dg-svg" id="dgSvg">
            <defs>
              <marker id="dgArrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"></path>
              </marker>
            </defs>
            <g id="dgEdgeLayer"></g>
          </svg>
          <div id="dgNodeLayer"></div>
        </div>
        <input type="text" id="dgEdgeLabelInput" class="dg-edge-input" autocomplete="off" hidden>
        <div id="dgContextMenu" class="dg-context-menu" hidden></div>
      </div>

      <p class="boe-hint dg-hint">Drag "+ Box", "+ Container", or "+ Text" onto the canvas, or click any of them to add. "+ Component" drops a pre-labeled icon: client and identity, compute, data, messaging and network, or AI. A container is a large frame: drop boxes (or other containers, nested as deep as you like) inside it and it carries them when it moves. Drag from a box's edge dot to connect it to another box. Double-click a box or a connection to rename it. Click to select, then press Delete to remove, Ctrl/Cmd+D to duplicate, Ctrl/Cmd+Z to undo. Right-click a box for more actions, including its shape (rect, rounded, diamond, or borderless text), an icon, and a color. Right-click a connection to set its line style, arrow direction, or an animated flow. Shift-click to select several boxes, then Group to move them as one, Ungroup to split them apart again. To Front and To Back change which box sits on top when they overlap. Scroll to zoom, drag empty space to pan. Saved to this browser automatically, and Export/Import JSON move a diagram between browsers or back it up.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
.boe-root { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--headline-color); font-size: 14px; }

.boe-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
.boe-blurb { font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 300; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--card-desc); max-width: 52ch; margin: 0; }
.boe-reset {
  font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--tone-bad); background: transparent; border: 1px solid var(--card-border);
  padding: 6px 12px; cursor: pointer; flex-shrink: 0; transition: border-color .4s ease;
}
.boe-reset:hover { border-color: var(--tone-bad); }

.boe-tabs { display: flex; gap: 4px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--card-border); }
.boe-tabbtn {
  font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--card-desc); background: transparent; border: 0; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
  padding: 8px 4px; margin-bottom: -1px; cursor: pointer; transition: color .3s ease, border-color .3s ease;
}
.boe-tabbtn + .boe-tabbtn { margin-left: 18px; }
.boe-tabbtn:hover { color: var(--headline-color); }
.boe-tabbtn.active { color: var(--card-label); border-bottom-color: var(--card-label); }
/* Diagram is a different kind of tool from the four numeric calculators before
   it (a canvas, not a form), set apart with a divider rather than blending
   into the same run of tabs. */
.boe-tabbtn[data-tab="diagram"] { margin-left: 30px; position: relative; }
.boe-tabbtn[data-tab="diagram"]::before {
  content: ""; position: absolute; left: -16px; top: 4px; bottom: 6px; width: 1px; background: var(--card-border);
